1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">Given two partially overlapping images and a
keypoint in the first,</font>

2
00:00:04,879 --> 00:00:07,414
<font color="#FFFFFF">where does its match land in the second?</font>

3
00:00:07,494 --> 00:00:11,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">Local feature matching only answers when the point
is visible.</font>

4
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:14,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">But humans can also guess, or hallucinate,</font>

5
00:00:14,291 --> 00:00:19,244
<font color="#FFFFFF">where an occluded or out of frame point should be,
using geometric reasoning.</font>

6
00:00:19,324 --> 00:00:23,361
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, Visual Correspondence Hallucination
from ICLR 2022,</font>

7
00:00:23,441 --> 00:00:29,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">trains a single network called NeurHal to output a
peaked probability distribution over</font>

8
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:32,381
<font color="#FFFFFF">the correspondent's location whether it is visible,</font>

9
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:34,930
<font color="#FFFFFF">occluded, or outside the field of view,</font>

10
00:00:35,010 --> 00:00:39,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">and shows this makes absolute camera pose
estimation far more robust.</font>

11
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:45,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">Establishing correspondences between two
overlapping images underlies much of computer vision,</font>

12
00:00:45,890 --> 00:00:48,135
<font color="#FFFFFF">from localization to reconstruction.</font>

13
00:00:48,215 --> 00:00:53,883
<font color="#FFFFFF">But local feature matching only works when a
keypoint is actually visible in both images.</font>

14
00:00:53,963 --> 00:00:56,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">When the correspondent is occluded,</font>

15
00:00:56,223 --> 00:01:00,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">or when it falls outside the field of view of the
second image,</font>

16
00:01:00,291 --> 00:01:05,378
<font color="#FFFFFF">these methods have nothing to say, and they simply
treat those regions as noise.</font>

17
00:01:05,458 --> 00:01:09,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">So whenever two images share little overlap,
matching breaks down.</font>

18
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:12,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">Humans do not give up in these situations.</font>

19
00:01:12,846 --> 00:01:15,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">Faced with an occluded or out of frame point,</font>

20
00:01:15,874 --> 00:01:21,109
<font color="#FFFFFF">a person reasons about the geometry of the scene
and predicts, or hallucinates,</font>

21
00:01:21,189 --> 00:01:22,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the match should be.</font>

22
00:01:22,939 --> 00:01:27,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classical vision does use geometric reasoning, but
only after the fact,</font>

23
00:01:27,716 --> 00:01:31,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">to filter out bad matches with models like epipolar
geometry.</font>

24
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:37,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">No prior method actually predicts correspondences
at locations that are not covisible.</font>

25
00:01:37,607 --> 00:01:40,420
<font color="#FFFFFF">That gap is exactly what this work targets.</font>

26
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:46,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper introduces the task of visual
correspondence hallucination and a network to solve it,</font>

27
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:49,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">called NeurHal, for Neural Hallucinations.</font>

28
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:53,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">Given a pair of overlapping images and keypoints in
the source image,</font>

29
00:01:54,017 --> 00:01:56,183
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeurHal outputs, for each keypoint,</font>

30
00:01:56,263 --> 00:02:01,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">a probability distribution over its correspondent's
location in the target image,</font>

31
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:06,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">whether that location is visible, occluded, or
outside the field of view.</font>

32
00:02:06,143 --> 00:02:11,389
<font color="#FFFFFF">This unifies three tasks the authors name
identifying, inpainting, and outpainting.</font>

33
00:02:11,469 --> 00:02:14,982
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because learning to hallucinate is unexplored territory,</font>

34
00:02:15,062 --> 00:02:18,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">they first analyze what makes the task distinctive,</font>

35
00:02:18,334 --> 00:02:22,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">and let that analysis drive the choice of loss and
architecture.</font>

36
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,301
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how NeurHal works.</font>

37
00:02:24,381 --> 00:02:29,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">A siamese convolutional backbone turns both images
into dense descriptor maps.</font>

38
00:02:29,303 --> 00:02:32,946
<font color="#FFFFFF">To allow correspondents outside the target's field
of view,</font>

39
00:02:33,026 --> 00:02:36,038
<font color="#FFFFFF">the target map is padded with a learnable vector,</font>

40
00:02:36,118 --> 00:02:39,193
<font color="#FFFFFF">initializing descriptors beyond the image borders.</font>

41
00:02:39,273 --> 00:02:46,324
<font color="#FFFFFF">A cross attention backbone with positional encoding
then lets source and target descriptors exchange information,</font>

42
00:02:46,404 --> 00:02:53,076
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is what enables the network to hallucinate
peaked distributions for occluded and out of frame points.</font>

43
00:02:53,156 --> 00:02:58,503
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each keypoint's feature is convolved over the
target features and passed through a two</font>

44
00:02:58,583 --> 00:03:01,153
<font color="#FFFFFF">D softmax to produce a correspondence map.</font>

45
00:03:01,233 --> 00:03:04,939
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training minimizes a sum of Neural Reprojection
Error terms,</font>

46
00:03:05,019 --> 00:03:08,789
<font color="#FFFFFF">the negative log likelihood of the true reprojected
location,</font>

47
00:03:08,869 --> 00:03:13,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no covisibility assumption and no need to
label points as visible or hidden.</font>

48
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:18,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is evaluated across both indoor and
outdoor domains.</font>

49
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:23,915
<font color="#FFFFFF">Indoors, NeurHal is trained on ScanNet and also
tested on the NYU Depth dataset;</font>

50
00:03:23,995 --> 00:03:28,195
<font color="#FFFFFF">outdoors, it is trained on MegaDepth and tested on
ETH three D.</font>

51
00:03:28,275 --> 00:03:33,426
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, every evaluation uses scenes that were
never seen during training,</font>

52
00:03:33,506 --> 00:03:36,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the results measure genuine generalization.</font>

53
00:03:36,632 --> 00:03:38,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the downstream application,</font>

54
00:03:38,738 --> 00:03:44,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">absolute camera pose estimation is measured on
twenty five hundred source and target image</font>

55
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:47,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">pairs from held out ScanNet scenes.</font>

56
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:50,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments confirm both research questions.</font>

57
00:03:50,609 --> 00:03:56,237
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeurHal successfully hallucinates correspondences
on scenes it never saw during training,</font>

58
00:03:56,317 --> 00:04:02,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">for occluded points that must be inpainted and for
out of frame points that must be outpainted,</font>

59
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:07,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">with prediction errors concentrated well below what
random guessing would give.</font>

60
00:04:07,477 --> 00:04:10,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">State of the art matching methods like LoFTR, DRC Net,</font>

61
00:04:10,940 --> 00:04:15,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">and S two D Net do poorly on inpainting and
essentially cannot outpaint at all,</font>

62
00:04:16,007 --> 00:04:19,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">since they only ever search inside the image boundaries.</font>

63
00:04:19,598 --> 00:04:22,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on absolute camera pose estimation,</font>

64
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:26,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeurHal is correct far more often than any
competitor on low overlap pairs,</font>

65
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:30,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">precisely because it is the only method that can outpaint.</font>

66
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:35,944
<font color="#FFFFFF">An ablation on ScanNet isolates what each task
contributes to pose robustness.</font>

67
00:04:36,024 --> 00:04:40,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training NeurHal on all three tasks together,
identification, inpainting,</font>

68
00:04:40,792 --> 00:04:43,325
<font color="#FFFFFF">and outpainting, gives the best results.</font>

69
00:04:43,405 --> 00:04:50,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key finding is that adding the outpainting task
is what drives the improvement on low overlap pairs,</font>

70
00:04:50,198 --> 00:04:54,428
<font color="#FFFFFF">where most correspondents fall outside the target's
field of view.</font>

71
00:04:54,508 --> 00:04:56,453
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adding inpainting, by contrast,</font>

72
00:04:56,533 --> 00:05:00,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">brings little additional benefit to camera pose
estimation.</font>

73
00:05:00,386 --> 00:05:06,119
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here a pose counts as correct when rotation error
is under twenty degrees and translation</font>

74
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:08,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">error under one point five meters.</font>

75
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:10,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers anchor the setup.</font>

76
00:05:10,696 --> 00:05:16,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pose estimates are judged correct under a rotation
threshold of twenty degrees and a translation</font>

77
00:05:16,817 --> 00:05:18,968
<font color="#FFFFFF">threshold of one point five meters,</font>

78
00:05:19,048 --> 00:05:22,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">measured over twenty five hundred held out ScanNet
image pairs.</font>

79
00:05:23,065 --> 00:05:27,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeurHal produces deliberately low resolution
correspondence maps,</font>

80
00:05:27,209 --> 00:05:31,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">using an effective stride of eight and an output
ratio of fifty percent,</font>

81
00:05:31,799 --> 00:05:38,477
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a six hundred forty by four hundred eighty
target yields a one hundred sixty by one hundred twenty map.</font>

82
00:05:38,557 --> 00:05:43,322
<font color="#FFFFFF">And across all the methods tested, NeurHal is the
only one able to outpaint,</font>

83
00:05:43,402 --> 00:05:47,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">to place correspondents beyond the target image's borders.</font>

84
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:49,628
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple but powerful.</font>

85
00:05:49,708 --> 00:05:55,782
<font color="#FFFFFF">A single network can learn to hallucinate keypoint
correspondences whether the match is visible,</font>

86
00:05:55,862 --> 00:05:58,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">occluded, or entirely outside the field of view,</font>

87
00:05:58,939 --> 00:06:05,206
<font color="#FFFFFF">and this ability makes absolute camera pose
estimation much more robust when images barely overlap.</font>

88
00:06:05,286 --> 00:06:10,399
<font color="#FFFFFF">In effect, NeurHal reframes non covisible regions
not as noise to be thrown away,</font>

89
00:06:10,479 --> 00:06:14,630
<font color="#FFFFFF">but as signal to be predicted through learned
geometric reasoning.</font>
